Chapter 27 – Part 4
Curtis shook his head once. Small. Final.
“I need the person known as Mikado. The one with a fire burning underneath his mask. Who you choose to be with your name is up to you. I came to you. I had only assumed you were the leader based off observations.”
The landing gave Mikado his lungs back.
Assumed. Not I have the logins. Not confess. Observations — class, jealousy, the way Masaomi flinched at a color, the way a quiet boy from outside the city watched everything like he was building a map.
The fire line landed harder than the Dollars question. Somebody had seen the thing he hid under polite and Anri-chan and cheap tea.
Mikado’s whisper went ragged with relief he did not want to show.
“…You came to me.” He tasted it. “Not the color. Me.”
He looked at the sliver of apartment door.
“Then that’s what you get. Mikado. The door. The kettle. The dork who will lie to a company if they knock. If the other name is real, that’s mine to carry. Don’t make me pick it in a stairwell while she’s listening for footsteps.”
A beat. The fire, brief and honest:
“You weren’t wrong about the mask. You might not be wrong about the rest. I’m not confirming a rumor for you. I’m telling you I’ll keep her. That’s the person you asked for.”
He straightened off the wall.
“Go take the streets. Come back in and say goodbye so she doesn’t think I bought you. And Nightstar—” His mouth twitched, almost a smile, almost sick. “Your observations are scary. Please don’t practice them on Kida next. He already thinks the city’s trying to eat me.”
On a narrow landing, the founder got to stay a rumor.
Mikado Ryuugamine was chosen as Mikado.
That, somehow,
was heavier than being called a leader
and lighter than being caught.
Curtis turned toward the door like the briefing was done.
Then, quiet enough that only the landing got it:
“Thanks, TarouTanaka.”
Mikado stopped breathing.
The handle sat in the air between them — the joke name, the founder name, the one Masaomi did not have, the one Izaya would sell a district to pin to a face.
Observations.
Right.
Mikado’s whisper came out ruined.
“…You—” His back found the wall again. “You said you assumed.”
Color left his face and came back wrong.
“That’s not a guess you get from watching me flinch at Kadota. That’s the chat. That’s my—” He cut himself off so hard his teeth clicked. Looked at the apartment slit. Looked at Curtis like the uniform had grown a second mouth. “How long. Don’t you dare say observations again.”
A swallow that hurt.
“Izaya doesn’t know. You said Izaya doesn’t know. If you can say that name in my stairwell, the harmless mask is already paper.” His voice dropped to almost nothing. “I’m still onboard. She’s still safe. But you don’t get to thank me with that word like it’s a nickname. That’s my throat, Nightstar.”
On a narrow landing, the assumption completed itself.
Mikado Ryuugamine heard his other name out loud
for the first time from a living mouth.
Curtis Nightstar had needed Mikado.
He had just proven
he already had TarouTanaka too.
Curtis kept it almost kind. Almost.
“How often do you think I’m actually sleeping in class? You have a bad habit of not turning your brightness down. I saw the handle when you tried to close it when Masaomi walked up. I didn’t think nothing of it back then. That was our first day.”
Mikado’s soul tried to leave through the grimy window.
First day. Introductions. Seiji walking out. Curtis half-dead at a desk. Anri. Rio. And Mikado, in the back, brightness up like a lighthouse, slamming a chat closed because Kida’s shadow had crossed the aisle.
He had thought the foreigner was asleep.
He had thought the foreigner was always asleep.
“…You’re kidding.” The whisper cracked into a hiss. “That was— I was checking if Kanra was being Kanra. That’s not— Nightstar, that’s my phone. In class. On day one.”
He pressed the heels of his hands into his eyes.
“I dim it now. I do. You were drooling on the desk. I thought you were a tall guy who missed the last train. Not a person who files handles.” A miserable little laugh. “Masaomi sat down and I thought I was smooth. I have never been smooth in my life.”
The fear changed shape. Not he’s been in the chat hunting me. Worse, in a way. He lives in the same room as me and watches when I think no one is watching.
Mikado dropped his hands.
“Okay. Brightness. First day. Observations on top. I hate how much that makes sense.” He pointed a weak finger at Curtis’s chest. “If you ever say TarouTanaka in front of Kida I will deny you until I die. I’m still the door. I’m still onboard. Just— next time you ‘sleep’ in class, warn me so I can throw the phone out the window first.”
On a narrow landing, the myth shrank to a lit screen.
Mikado Ryuugamine had not been hacked.
He had been sitting next to a boy who only looked unconscious —
and Curtis Nightstar had been collecting
since the first bell.
Curtis shook his head. No knife in it this time.
“I’m not going to tell Masaomi. That’s not why I’m here. Why would I do something cruel to you? I don’t hate you and I don’t even dislike you, Mikado.”
The landing let Mikado put one piece of himself back.
He stared at Curtis like the sentence needed a receipt.
“…You just said my throat-name after telling me you clocked my phone on day one. Forgive me if I don’t dislike you sounds like a setup.”
Then the fight leaked out of him. He slouched against the wall, first-year again.
“Okay. You don’t tell Kida. Thank you. He would smile and then he would not sleep and then he would try to drag me out of the city with both hands.” A breath. “I don’t dislike you either. That’s the annoying part. I wanted to. It would have been easier. Class rep. Anri. The way you walk into rooms like you already paid for them.”
He glanced at the door.
“You’re still scary. You’re just not cruel. I can work with that.” A weaker, realer look. “Come say goodbye to her. If you vanish down the stairs she’ll think I bought the two minutes. I don’t want that version of me in her head.”
On a narrow landing, dislike failed to arrive.
Mikado Ryuugamine kept his other name
and his best friend
in two different pockets.
Curtis Nightstar had not come to be cruel.
That, for a boy who founded a color in secret,
was almost as dangerous as being known.
Curtis rubbed the back of his neck. The street-voice dropped a notch.
“I need you at school later too. Masaomi and Anri will ask questions. We need to be on the DL with this. Anri is in the loop somewhat. But I don’t wanna drag anyone else into this. I feel guilty for asking you. So I’m sorry for dragging you into this.”
Mikado’s first thought was not Yagiri.
It was Anri’s name in Curtis’s mouth, somewhat.
What does she know. How much. Why him and not me. Why always him.
He packed that away because a girl with a reconstructed neck was listening through cheap wood.
“…School. Right. I show up. I look like a dork. I don’t look like I spent the morning hiding a missing classmate.” He nodded once, too many times. “Kida asks, I change the subject. Anri asks…” His jaw worked. “If she’s somewhat in the loop, you need to tell me which parts so I don’t lie her into a corner. I won’t out Mika. I also won’t pretend Anri is a stranger to this if you’ve already trusted her with pieces.”
The apology landed in a softer place than TarouTanaka had.
Mikado looked at his shoes.
“Don’t apologize like you stole me. I said onboard. I meant it. Guilt doesn’t keep her safer.” A thin breath. “Just come to class. Sleep at your desk like usual. I’ll keep my brightness down. We’ll be boring. Boring is the point.”
He tipped his chin at the door.
“Now go tell her you’re sorry for the stairwell too. She asked for a person goodbye. I can do DL. I can’t do her thinking you handed her off like a file.”
On a narrow landing, the cover story got a school bell.
Mikado Ryuugamine would sit in class
and look harmless on purpose.
Curtis Nightstar had said I’m sorry
like dragging a good guy into a company war
was the part that actually bothered him
Curtis caught the flinch when Anri’s name landed. He filed it and answered it anyway.
“She knows I found Mika. She doesn’t know Yagiri is involved. She doesn’t know Mika went through facial reconstruction.”
Mikado’s shoulders dropped a fraction. Somewhat had edges now. He could work with edges.
Then Curtis’s phone lit the stairwell.
The name on the screen was not Anri. Not Celty. Not Rio.
Namie Yagiri.
“Fuuuck.”
Mikado leaned in on reflex. Curtis turned the glass against his chest.
“Everything okay?”
“No, Mikado. I will pay you back. I gotta go.”
He was already moving — two stairs at a time, braids swinging, Raira uniform eating the morning. The landing door below clapped. Namie Yagiri kept ringing in his pocket like a company that had found a new number to collect.
Mikado stood alone with a locked secret and an unlocked apartment.
He smoothed his shirt. Knocked once on his own door so he would not walk in like a thief. Pushed it open.
Mika was where he had left the idea of her: small room, pink rose, eyes that had learned tables.
“…Hi.” He bowed because his body remembered manners when his brain did not. “I’m Mikado Ryuugamine. Anri’s friend. Curtis’s— classmate.” The title felt thin. “You’re safe here. I mean that. There’s tea. It’s not good tea. You can have the futon. I’ll take the floor. I won’t ask about your neck unless you want me to.”
Mika looked past him at the empty landing.
“He ran.”
“He said he’d pay me back.” Mikado winced at how that sounded. “Not— not for you. For the trouble. Someone called. He didn’t show me who.”
Mika’s mouth flattened.
“If that someone is a van, I’m leaving out the window.”
“It’s not a van. It’s a one-room and a dork. He said you could kick my ass. Please don’t. I just bought this kettle.”
A beat. Then, because the silence was worse:
“I’m onboard. That’s the word he used. I’m still that. Even if he just vanished down my stairs like a problem with a phone.”
On a cheap apartment floor, the courier left and the door stayed.
Namie Yagiri had reached Curtis Nightstar.
Mikado Ryuugamine introduced himself to Mika Harima
with bad tea and a promise
he had not known how expensive it would be
when he typed yes the night before.
Mika did not wait for the kettle.
She thumbed Curtis’s number with the rose in her other hand.
you didnt say bye
you owe me a kiss
Two floors of city and a ringing Yagiri name later, his reply landed without poetry.
thats fair.
Mika stared at the two words, then locked the phone like it might grin at her. “Don’t read into it,” she muttered at nobody. The receipt had grown interest.
Mikado pretended he had not seen her face do that. He made tea. Cheap leaves. Too much water. The apartment smelled like a first-year trying.
He wiped his hands on his pants and felt paper that did not belong there.
A wad. Thick. Not polite-thank-you money. The kind of fold you used when you did not have time to count.
Mikado sat down on his own floor.
“…How?”
The brush-past on the landing. Curtis’s shoulder. The I will pay you back already happening while Namie Yagiri lit up a screen.
“He did it when he brushed past me.”
Mikado turned the wad over like it might have a lecture written on the band. There wasn’t one. Just cash in a room that rented by the month and a kettle he had been proud of buying.
Conflict sat down next to him.
He clocks my chat handle. He drops a missing girl on my futon. He runs toward a last name that owns vans. He still isn’t rude. He still says I’m a good guy. He still slips money into my pocket like I’m not a joke.
Jealousy tried to stand up. It had Anri’s name. Class rep. The way Mika had held Curtis’s sleeve.
The wad made hating him feel cheap.
Mikado laughed once, helpless, and hid the money in a drawer under shirts so it would not look at him.
“Drink the tea,” he told Mika, voice almost steady. “It’s bad. He’ll come back. Or he won’t, and I’ll still be the door.”
On a cheap apartment floor, two receipts landed at once.
Mika Harima collected a promised kiss by text.
Mikado Ryuugamine held thanks he had not asked for
and found Curtis Nightstar
the hardest person in Ikebukuro to dislike
even when it would have been easier.
Curtis answered on the fourth ring because the name would not die politely.
“How come you didn’t answer my calls?” Namie’s voice arrived already mid-argument, clipped, expensive. “I found my brother. He got roughed up by Shizuo. Now I need your help with something else. Where are you? I will pick you up.”
Curtis stopped under a awning. Morning traffic. A vending machine humming like it had opinions.
“Holy shit. I’m fine, thank you. And I take two cream and two sugar with coffee.”
Silence on the line. The kind with teeth.
Namie Yagiri did not laugh.
“…Did you just give me a coffee order.”
“You asked where I was. I’m in a city. Coffee would help.”
“Seiji has a bruise the shape of a bartender and you’re joking like this is a date.” Paper moved on her end. A car door, maybe. “I am not your secretary and I am not bringing you a latte. I am bringing a car. Pin a location or I will have it found.”
Curtis watched a bus exhale at the curb.
“You can find a lot of things, Namie. That’s the problem.” He kept walking. Uniform. Braids. Calm voice that was not calm. “I’m not getting in a Yagiri car before breakfast. Text me what the something else is. If it’s your brother, take him to Shinra or a real hospital. If it’s the other kind of something, I’m not doing it in a parking garage.”
Namie’s pause went thinner.
“You think I called you for a courier job.” A short, unkind breath. “Shizuo put Seiji on the pavement for standing too close to the wrong rumor. I need someone who can stand in a room without becoming a specimen or a victim. That list is short. You are on it. Do not make me add ungrateful to your file.”
“Two cream,” Curtis said. “Two sugar. And I’m not pinning you my morning. Talk or I hang up and you can yell at the next person who answers.”
On an Ikebukuro sidewalk, a company reached out with a car.
Namie Yagiri had found her brother
and wanted Curtis Nightstar next.
He offered her coffee instead of a pin
because some pickups
were just vans with better upholstery.
Curtis walked until Mikado’s building was just another cheap box in the rearview of his head. Then he pinned a corner with a family restaurant and a bus stop. Neutral. Far.
Namie’s car was too clean for the hour. She popped the door like she owned the lane.
A paper cup hit his palm before his knee hit the seat.
“Jokes aren’t your strong suit, are they?” Curtis took a sip. Two cream. Two sugar. She had done it anyway. “But I appreciate the coffee. Now without it all in one sentence yelling at me. What do you need, Namie?”
She pulled into traffic. Sunglasses. The kind of posture that made other cars guess pharmaceutical.
“Seiji was with a girl. The Black Rider came down on them. Shizuo was already in the street being Shizuo. They ran. Seiji, being Seiji, stabbed Shizuo with a pen.” Namie’s mouth pinched. “Shizuo reacted. I found my brother on the pavement. The girl ran. I need her found.”
Curtis watched the city slide.
“Why would he try stabbing Shizuo? That’s punishment in itself.”
“Because my brother thinks love is a reason to be stupid.” Namie flicked the turn signal harder than it needed. “The rider wanted the girl or the thing near the girl. I don’t care which myth is true this morning. Seiji is bruised and talking in circles. The girl is gone. She is not a civilian who wandered into a bar fight. She is an asset that walked off a table.”
Curtis drank coffee and made his face a tourist.
“Asset.” He let the word sit like he did not already hate it. “So you want me to walk Ikebukuro looking for… what. A name? A face? A jacket?”
“A girl who should not have been able to get far. Dark hair. Young. She will look wrong if you stare too long. People who have been reconstructed do that.” Namie did not look at him. “You hear things. You stand in rooms other foreigners bounce out of. You pulled Anri Sonohara out of a teacher’s orbit and you did not die when Izaya put a knife in you. I am not asking you to be a hero. I am asking you to find her before the rider does. Or before Shizuo decides the next person holding a pen is also the problem.”
Curtis turned the cup in his hands.
“Reconstructed,” he repeated, dumb as a first-day transfer. “Like plastic surgery. Okay. I’ll keep an eye out. If I see a girl running from a headless motorcycle I’ll text you. I’m not kicking down doors for Yagiri Pharmaceuticals before I’ve finished this coffee.”
Namie’s jaw ticked.
“Play slow if you want. Just don’t play kind. Kindness loses girls in this city.”
On a clean seat in a dirty morning, Namie Yagiri hired a search
for a person already drinking bad tea
three neighborhoods behind them.
Curtis Nightstar played tourist
with two sugars in his mouth
and Mika Harima’s name
locked behind his teeth.
Curtis set the cup in the holder like he was done being recruited.
“You have your brother. What’s the point of some girl? Take your brother and do your thing.”
Namie’s hands clenched the wheel. Leather complained.
“He doesn’t see me that way.”
Curtis sighed. The coffee went bitter on the second sip.
“Yeah. You told me yesterday.” He watched a crosswalk instead of her face. “I’ll do what I can, Namie. But I’m not a trafficker.”
The car took a corner too neat.
Namie’s voice came out thin and expensive.
“I didn’t ask you to sell her. I asked you to find her. Seiji will tear this city into strips if he thinks she vanished into the rider or into a van that isn’t ours.” A swallow she would deny later. “He looks at her and he sees a person he chose. He looks at me and he sees a sister who signs papers. That is the point of the girl. She is the room he walks into. I am the hallway.”
She flicked a glance at Curtis. Sharp. Tired.
“You can hate the word asset. Fine. Hate it. Just understand I am trying to keep my brother from bleeding on Shizuo twice in one week. If you find her, you call me. If you decide to grow a conscience in the middle of Ikebukuro, do it after I have Seiji on a couch and not in a rumor.”
A red light. Her knuckles stayed white.
“And don’t you dare say that way back to me like it’s a joke. I already lived the sentence.”
On a clean morning road, Namie Yagiri admitted the hunt
was not only company.
Curtis Nightstar would look.
He would not traffic.
Those were not the same job —
and she knew it,
which was why her hands
would not unclench.
Curtis watched the light go green.
“Wanna go on a date sometime?”
Namie’s face went red so fast the sunglasses looked useless.
“The hell you say?!”
The car drifted half a lane. She yanked it back like the steering wheel had insulted her family.
“I am looking for a missing girl. My brother has a bartender-shaped bruise. You just called me a trafficker with better grammar and now you—” She pointed at him without taking her hand off the wheel. “That is not how this conversation works. I did not give you coffee so you could audition.”
Curtis drank anyway. Calm. Almost curious.
“You clenched when I said take your brother and do your thing. You look like you haven’t been asked as a person in a while. It was a question. Not a contract.”
“It was insane.” Namie’s ears were still pink. She stared hard at traffic. “I don’t date search-parties. I don’t date foreigners who joke about cream and sugar while my company is leaking. And I don’t—”
She stopped. The that way from a minute ago sat between them, ugly and honest.
“…Don’t do that again while I’m driving.”
Not a no.
Not a yes.
On a too-clean car seat, Namie Yagiri blushed at the wrong sentence.
Curtis Nightstar had asked for a date
the same way he asked for coffee —
and Ikebukuro, which collected bad timing,
filed another complication
next to a girl who was already found.
Curtis sipped his coffee.
“I’ll file that as a maybe, then.”
“Shut up.”
Namie said it to the windshield. Pink still sat high on her ears. The turn signal ticked too loud.
She did not throw him out. She also did not say no a second time. The car kept eating blocks like the city could outrun the sentence.
“Find the girl,” she muttered. “Use your little street-eyes. Text me if you see her. Do not text me maybe.”
Curtis lifted the cup in a small salute and watched Ikebukuro go by.
On a morning that already had too many files,
Namie Yagiri drove with her mouth shut
and her ears still pink.
Curtis Nightstar drank two sugars
and did not mention Mika Harima even once.
Curtis glanced over. Not teasing this time. Checking.
“C’mon. You’re beautiful. Don’t act like no one’s ever asked you on a date.” A beat. His eyebrows went up. “Wait… you’re kidding me.”
Namie’s throat worked.
“I didn’t say that.”
She had not had to. The silence after shut up had already confessed it.
“People ask Namie Yagiri for signatures. For access. For my brother’s location. For a discount on things that should not have a price.” Her voice stayed clipped so it would not shake. “They do not ask me if I want dinner like I am not a last name. So you can put your you’re kidding me back in the cup.”
A red light. She would not look at him.
“And don’t call me beautiful in a company car while I am trying to recover a girl. It makes you sound kind. Kind is inconvenient.”
Curtis drank. Filed it next to maybe.
“Alright,” he said. “I’ll stop saying it in the car.”
Not the same as stopping.
On a morning commute that was not a commute,
Namie Yagiri learned the question had been real
and hated that her face still knew how to go warm.
Curtis Nightstar had met a lot of monsters.
A woman no one asked out
was a different kind of locked door.
Curtis turned the cup in his hands.
“I can be bolder. Let’s go to dinner, then a shooting range to blow off some steam after we find your missing girl.”
Namie’s mouth opened. Closed.
“…Maybe.”
It came out like a paper cut. Small. Honest. She stared at the next light as if it had asked her first.
Curtis smiled. Not the street-read. Softer. He said her name the way a last name wanted to be a person.
“A maybe works for me, Miss Namie.”
Her fingers jumped on the wheel.
She had been called Yagiri. Namie-san. That woman from the company. Not Miss Namie like the words had been practiced for her spine.
“Stop.” It had no heat. “Do that after the girl is in a room I can lock. Do not do that while I am driving and Seiji is somewhere icing a bruise.”
A breath.
“Dinner is public. A range is noisy. Both are… fine. Maybe. If you actually look. If you don’t vanish into your little wanderer act and text me poetry.”
Curtis sipped.
“I’ll look.”
He already had.
He just would not look where she thought.
On a too-clean morning ride, maybe grew a dinner
and a trigger pull.
Namie Yagiri heard Miss Namie
and filed it somewhere Seiji would never be allowed to see.
Curtis Nightstar smiled into his coffee and kept Mika Harima off the map.
One block short of anywhere useful, Curtis popped the door.
“It was a pleasure as always, Miss Namie.”
He left the empty cup in the holder like a receipt.
Namie watched him unfold onto the sidewalk — Raira uniform, braids, coffee-calm — and hated that the goodbye sounded practiced.
“Nightstar.” He looked back. She kept her sunglasses on so the pink would not count. “Look for the girl. Not for another maybe. I will text you a description. If Shizuo is in the street, walk around him. I am not explaining two bodies to my uncle today.”
Curtis tipped two fingers off his brow.
“I’ll keep an eye out.”
The door shut. The clean car slid into traffic with a woman who had said maybe and already wanted the word back.
Curtis waited until the taillights were just city. Then he turned the opposite direction of Mikado’s building and started walking like a student late for a bell.
On a random Ikebukuro corner, the pickup ended.
Namie Yagiri drove toward a brother
and a missing asset.
Curtis Nightstar said pleasure
with Mika already found
and a date filed under after —
as if after would ever be that simple.